n management. These favorable factors were offset by soft international demand. Changes in foreign currency favorably impacted net sales by $70 million, or approximately 1%.\nGross profit increased by approximately $170 million, or 9% compared to the prior year. Gross margin improved to 36.4% as compared with 34.0% in the prior year, reflecting the impact of higher net sales and recognition of a refund of approximately $100 million related to IEEPA Tariffs that were expensed in 2025. Gross profit was also favorably impacted by net pricing actions, including the $25 million contribution related to customer programs discussed above and gross productivity, which were partially offset by inflationary pressures. Changes in foreign currency exchange rates favorably impacted gross profit by $10 million, approximately 1%.\nNotable items, other than those noted above, impacting operating income for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025 were as follows (in millions):\nNine Months Ended September 30,\n2026 2025\n"}
